"vocabulary":{"evidence_type":"ECO (Evidence & Conclusion Ontology)","study_design":"MeSH Publication Types","strength":"Oxford CEBM 2009 levels","structure":"SEPIO assertion / evidence line / evidence item"},"search_url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=beta-Phellandrene%5BAll%20Fields%5D"},"summary":null,"evidence":"Measured in 4 of 6 MONDAYS hemp SKUs at 0.06–0.41% of total volatiles (0.012–0.082mg per chew).","trials":{"total":0,"matched_loosely":0,"studies":[],"source":"ClinicalTrials.gov","search_url":"https://clinicaltrials.gov/search?intr=beta-Phellandrene"},"is_terpenoid":true,"consumer_summary":{"model":"gemini-3.1-pro-preview","generated":"2026-09-11","what_it_is":"Beta-phellandrene is a cyclic monoterpene, a volatile aromatic molecule that plants produce to interact with their environment. It contributes to the fresh, peppery, and woody scents found in many common herbs and trees. You likely encounter it in daily life when chopping fresh kitchen herbs, walking through a pine forest, or using essential oils.","found_in":"This terpene is widely distributed in nature, appearing prominently in culinary herbs like dill, ginger, and coriander, as well as in parsley and garlic. It is also a key component in the aromatic profiles of tea tree and coniferous trees like pine and spruce.","why_researchers_care":"Researchers are drawn to beta-phellandrene for its broad potential in plant defense and human health, particularly exploring its antimicrobial and antioxidant properties. The strongest evidence for these effects currently comes from human observational studies (Oxford level 2b). Scientists are also actively investigating how to sustainably produce this useful terpene by engineering cyanobacteria, opening up new possibilities for natural product manufacturing.","structure_function":[{"claim_id":"antimicrobial","statement":"May support the body's natural defenses against microbes.","evidence_level":"human_observational","basis":"Based on 27 papers, inclu